---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import MoreInCategory from "../components/MoreInCategory.astro";
import { authorSchema, authorName } from "../data/authors";
import AuthorAvatar from "../components/AuthorAvatar.astro";
import QuickPicks from "../components/QuickPicks.astro";
import PlanTheJob from "../components/PlanTheJob.astro";
import AuthorBio from "../components/AuthorBio.astro";
import { amazonLink } from "../data/affiliate";
import TrustBadge from "../components/TrustBadge.astro";
import SocialProof from "../components/SocialProof.astro";
import productImages from "../data/product-images.json";
import { pageDates } from "../data/dates";

const moreInCategory = [
  {
    "title": "Best Deck Stains",
    "url": "/best-deck-stain-reviews/"
  },
  {
    "title": "Best Deck Stain Brushes",
    "url": "/best-deck-stain-brush/"
  },
  {
    "title": "Brush vs Roller vs Sprayer for Deck Stain",
    "url": "/brush-vs-roller-vs-sprayer-deck-stain/"
  },
  {
    "title": "Best Paint Sprayers",
    "url": "/best-paint-sprayer/"
  },
  {
    "title": "How to Stain a Deck",
    "url": "/how-to-stain-a-deck/"
  }
];
const dates = pageDates(Astro.url.pathname, { published: "2026-11-05", modified: "2026-11-05" });
const title = "6 Best Deck Stain Sprayers in 2026: Pump, Airless and Which Stain Each Suits";
const description =
  "The best deck stain sprayers: pump sprayers for penetrating stain, airless with low-pressure tips for solid stain, why HVLP is slow, and how to stop overspray.";

const PRODUCTS = [
  {
    "id": "chapin-25012-clean-n-seal",
    "name": "Chapin 25012 1-Gallon Clean 'N Seal Poly Deck Sprayer",
    "category": "Pump Sprayer, 1 Gallon, Transparent and Semi-Transparent Stain",
    "badge": "Best Overall",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "pros": [
      "Built and labeled by Chapin for deck cleaners and transparent stains and sealers",
      "Three poly fan nozzles, fine, medium and coarse, instead of a garden cone nozzle",
      "Total Control spray shield to keep stain off siding and plants",
      "Funnel-top translucent tank makes it easy to see the level and clean out",
      "Light enough to carry along a railing with one hand"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "1-gallon tank means frequent refills on a large deck",
      "Not for solid stain or thick semi-solid products",
      "Short 34-inch hose and 12-inch wand limit reach under rails"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The sprayer most homeowners with a penetrating or semi-transparent stain should buy: cheap, purpose-built for stain, and easy to clean.",
    "description": "Most of the deck stain sold for DIY use is a thin, penetrating oil or semi-transparent product, and for that kind of stain an expensive airless sprayer is overkill. A pump sprayer with the right nozzle does the job at a fraction of the cost and with far less overspray, and the Chapin 25012 is the one we would buy. Chapin sells it specifically for deck cleaners and transparent stains and sealers, rather than as a garden sprayer that happens to work. The important difference is the nozzles: it comes with three poly fan nozzles in fine, medium and coarse patterns. A flat fan lays a stripe of stain along a board or down a baluster, which is far more controllable than the cone pattern of a lawn and garden sprayer. The Total Control spray shield is a small hood you hold between the nozzle and whatever you do not want stained, such as siding or a flower bed. The tank holds 1 gallon, with a funnel top and translucent sides, so you can see how much is left. Chapin lists a 34-inch hose and 12-inch extension wand. On a big deck the 1-gallon size means refilling often, and it will not handle solid stain. For a railing system and a penetrating stain, it is the best value here."
  },
  {
    "id": "chapin-26021xp-proseries",
    "name": "Chapin 26021XP ProSeries 2-Gallon Heavy-Duty Compression Sprayer",
    "category": "Pump Sprayer, 2 Gallon, Chemical-Resistant Seals",
    "badge": "Best 2-Gallon Pump Sprayer",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "pros": [
      "2-gallon tank halves the number of refills on a larger deck",
      "Chemical-resistant seals, listed as Viton by parts suppliers, stand up to solvent-based stains",
      "Pressure relief valve and an anti-clog filter",
      "Includes adjustable brass and poly nozzles, with a poly fan option",
      "48-inch hose gives more reach than the 1-gallon Chapin"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Sold as a multi-purpose sprayer, not stain-specific, so no spray shield in the box",
      "Heavier to carry when full than the 1-gallon model"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The pump sprayer to buy if your deck is large or you also want to use it for deck cleaner and brightener, and you want seals that survive oil-based stain.",
    "description": "The 1-gallon Chapin is ideal for railings, but on a large deck or a long fence the refills get old. The 26021XP is Chapin's ProSeries 2-gallon compression sprayer, and the reason it is here rather than a cheaper 2-gallon garden sprayer is the seals. Oil-based and solvent-based stains attack the standard rubber seals used in many lawn and garden sprayers, which then swell, leak and stop holding pressure. Chapin describes the 26021XP's seals as chemical-resistant, and parts suppliers list them as Viton, the material you want for solvent exposure. It also has a pressure relief valve, an anti-clog filter and a cushion-grip shut-off, with adjustable brass and poly nozzles included; retailer listings mention a poly fan nozzle as well, which is the one to use for stain. Retailers list a 48-inch hose, and the tank has a wide 4-inch opening for filling and cleaning. What it does not have is a spray shield, so pick up a piece of cardboard or a shield separately if you are working near siding. It is also the better choice if you plan to use one sprayer for cleaner, brightener and stain over the years, though you should clean it thoroughly between products. For a small deck, the 1-gallon model is easier to handle."
  },
  {
    "id": "wagner-control-pro-130",
    "name": "Wagner 0580678 Control Pro 130 High Efficiency Airless Sprayer",
    "category": "High Efficiency Airless (HEA), 1,600 PSI, 1.5 Gallon Tank",
    "badge": "Best Low-Overspray Airless",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "pros": [
      "Wagner's HEA design sprays at lower pressure than conventional airless, which cuts overspray",
      "Rated at 1,600 PSI and 0.24 GPM, enough for solid and semi-solid stain",
      "1.5-gallon pressurized tank lets you move around the deck without a bucket",
      "25-foot hose and 515 HEA tip included",
      "Accepts tips up to .015"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Much more expensive and more cleanup than a pump sprayer",
      "Overkill for thin penetrating stains, which a pump sprayer handles fine"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The airless to buy for solid or semi-solid stain on a deck next to the house, where overspray is the main worry.",
    "description": "If you are applying a solid or semi-solid stain to a large deck, railing and fence, a pump sprayer struggles and an airless becomes worth considering. The problem with a conventional airless near a house is overspray: at 2,000 to 3,000 PSI, a fine mist drifts well beyond the work area onto siding, windows and cars. Wagner's Control Pro line uses what it calls High Efficiency Airless, which runs at lower pressure, and the Control Pro 130 is rated at 1,600 PSI maximum and 0.24 gallons per minute. Wagner markets it as low-overspray, and the lower pressure is why. It sprays from a 1.5-gallon pressurized tank rather than drawing from a bucket, which is genuinely convenient on a deck because you can carry it along a railing, and it comes with a 25-foot hose and a 515 HEA tip. The maximum tip size is .015. For a deck, a smaller tip is often a better match for thinner stains. It is still an airless, so mask the house, keep the gun moving and back-brush everything. And if your stain is a thin penetrating oil, save your money and buy the Chapin."
  },
  {
    "id": "graco-magnum-x5",
    "name": "Graco Magnum X5 Airless Paint Sprayer 262800",
    "category": "Stand Airless, 3,000 PSI, Sprays from 1 or 5 Gallon Pail",
    "badge": "Best for Big Solid-Stain Jobs",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "pros": [
      "Graco rates it at 3,000 PSI and 0.27 GPM, the most output here",
      "Sprays directly from a 1 or 5-gallon pail, so no refilling a tank",
      "Adjustable pressure control, which matters for stain",
      "Supports up to 75 feet of hose for reaching around a large deck and fence",
      "Accepts Graco reversible tips, including low-pressure fine finish tips"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Most overspray of any sprayer here at full pressure",
      "Cleanup after every session takes real time",
      "Unnecessary for a small deck or penetrating stain"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "Buy it for a large deck, fence and railings in solid stain, or if you will also use it on house siding. Everyone else should buy a pump sprayer.",
    "description": "The Magnum X5 is the airless sprayer we would choose if the job is big: a large deck with railings, a long privacy fence, or a deck plus siding in solid stain. Graco rates it at 3,000 PSI maximum and 0.27 gallons per minute, with a 0.5 HP motor, and it draws from a 1 or 5-gallon pail through a suction tube, so you do not stop to refill. It ships with a 25-foot hose and a 515 tip, supports up to 75 feet of hose, and accepts tips up to .015. The adjustable pressure control is the feature that matters most for stain. Deck stains are much thinner than house paint, and spraying them at full pressure atomizes too much of the material into mist, which drifts and wastes stain. Turn the pressure down until the fan just stops showing tails, and consider fitting a low-pressure fine finish tip, which Graco says sprays at up to 50 percent lower pressure. Our general <a href=\"/best-paint-sprayer/\">paint sprayer guide</a> covers its bigger sibling, the Magnum X7, for whole-house painting. For deck stain alone, the X5 has all the output you need. It is still overkill for a penetrating stain on a 200 square foot deck, and it means flushing a pump, hose and gun every time you stop."
  },
  {
    "id": "graco-fflp310-tip",
    "name": "Graco FFLP310 RAC X Fine Finish Low Pressure Reversible Tip",
    "category": "Airless Spray Tip, 6 in. Fan, .010 Orifice",
    "badge": "Best Airless Upgrade",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "pros": [
      "Graco says FFLP tips spray at up to 50 percent lower pressure than standard tips",
      "Softer fan pattern with less overspray, which is the main problem with stain in an airless",
      "Small .010 orifice suits thin stain better than the .015 tip most sprayers ship with",
      "Reversible, so clogs clear by turning the tip",
      "Rated to 4,050 PSI"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Needs a RAC X tip guard; check your sprayer's guard before ordering",
      "A 6-inch fan is narrow for a deck floor, so you may want a wider FFLP size as well"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The cheapest way to make an airless sprayer behave better with deck stain. If you own or rent a Graco, buy one.",
    "description": "Most of the complaints about spraying deck stain through an airless come down to the tip. Airless sprayers ship with tips sized for house paint, typically a 515, which puts out a lot of material at high pressure. Deck stain is much thinner, so through a paint tip it sprays heavy, runs, and fogs. Graco's Fine Finish Low Pressure tips are designed for thinner materials and, by Graco's description, spray at up to 50 percent lower pressure with a softer fan and less overspray; retailers list stains among their intended uses. In Graco's numbering, a 310 gives roughly a 6-inch fan at 12 inches from the surface through a .010 orifice. That narrow, light pattern is well suited to balusters, rails and fence pickets, where a wide fan wastes most of the stain into the air. For deck boards, a wider FFLP tip in the same orifice size covers faster. The tip fits Graco's RAC X guard, and retailers list Magnum sprayers among compatible models, but check the guard on your sprayer before ordering. It is reversible, so a clog clears by rotating the tip and spraying briefly away from the work."
  },
  {
    "id": "wagner-flexio-595",
    "name": "Wagner FLEXiO 595 Handheld HVLP Paint and Stain Sprayer",
    "category": "Handheld HVLP, 1.5 Quart Cup",
    "badge": "Best HVLP for Railings",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "pros": [
      "Low-pressure HVLP spray gives the least overspray of any powered sprayer here",
      "Includes the iSpray nozzle for broad surfaces and a Detail Finish nozzle for fine work",
      "Wagner sells it as a paint and stain sprayer",
      "Fine control on balusters, spindles and lattice"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "1.5-quart cup needs constant refilling on a deck",
      "Far slower than a pump or airless sprayer on a floor",
      "Turbine in the gun is tiring to hold for long sessions"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "Worth buying only for intricate railings, lattice or furniture near the house, or if you already own one. Not a deck floor tool.",
    "description": "HVLP sprayers use a turbine to move a large volume of air at low pressure, which atomizes the material gently and keeps overspray to a minimum. That makes them excellent for furniture, cabinets and fine finish work, and Wagner sells the FLEXiO 595 as a paint and stain sprayer. It comes with two nozzles: the iSpray nozzle for broader surfaces and a Detail Finish nozzle for fine work, with a 1.5-quart cup. On a deck, its strength is control. On a railing system with dozens of spindles, decorative lattice or a pergola close to windows, an HVLP gives you a soft, even coat with very little drift. Its weakness is everything else. The 1.5-quart cup empties quickly, the spray rate is a fraction of an airless, and with the turbine in the gun you are holding a few pounds at arm's length the whole time. On a deck floor it is the slowest option on this page, slower than a pad on a pole. Buy it if the railing detail is the hard part of your job and you will use the sprayer indoors afterwards; otherwise the Chapin does more for much less money."
  }
];

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 1,
          name: "Home",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 2,
          name: "Best Lists",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-of/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 3,
          name: "Best Deck Stain Sprayer",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-deck-stain-sprayer/",
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What is the best sprayer for deck stain?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "For transparent and semi-transparent stains, which are most deck stains, a pump sprayer with a flat fan nozzle such as the Chapin 25012 is the best choice. It is cheap, controllable and produces little overspray. For solid stain over a large area, an airless sprayer with adjustable pressure or a low-pressure tip, such as the Graco Magnum X5, is better."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can you use a pump sprayer for deck stain?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Yes. A pump sprayer is the most common and often the best way to spray penetrating deck stain. Use a flat fan nozzle rather than a cone, choose chemical-resistant seals for oil-based stain, and flush the sprayer right after use. Pump sprayers struggle with solid stain, which is usually too thick for their nozzles."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is an airless sprayer good for deck stain?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "It can be, especially for solid stain on large decks and fences, but it needs setting up for stain. Turn the pressure down, use a smaller tip than the paint tip it ships with, and consider a low-pressure fine finish tip. Mask the house carefully, because airless overspray drifts, and back-brush everything behind the gun."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can you spray deck stain with an HVLP sprayer?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "You can, and HVLP gives the least overspray and finest control, which suits spindles, lattice and furniture. But it is slow and most consumer units have a cup of about a quart, so it is impractical for a deck floor. Use it for detailed railings and use a pad, brush or pump sprayer for the boards."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Do you have to back-brush after spraying deck stain?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Yes. Spraying puts stain on the surface; back-brushing works it into the grain, spreads out puddles, pulls excess from the gaps and evens the color. Without it, heavy areas stay tacky and thin areas fail early. On anything larger than a small railing, spraying and brushing is easiest as a two-person job."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What tip size should I use to spray deck stain?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "For thin penetrating stains through an airless, a small orifice such as .009 to .011 works better than the .015 tips most sprayers ship with. A narrow fan suits balusters and pickets; a wider fan suits floor boards. Low-pressure tips like Graco's FFLP line reduce overspray further. For solid stain, follow the stain maker's tip recommendation."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How do I stop overspray when spraying a deck?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Mask the house wall, windows and the ground below the deck before you start. Use a pump sprayer with a spray shield near siding, or an airless at low pressure with a low-pressure tip. Spray on a calm day, aim down and away from the house, and work from the wall outward so you never reach over wet stain."
          }
        }
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      author: authorSchema(Astro.url.pathname),
      datePublished: dates.published,
      dateModified: dates.modified,
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "The Honest Reviewers" },
    },
    {
      "@type": "ItemList",
      name: title,
      numberOfItems: PRODUCTS.length,
      itemListElement: PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => ({
        "@type": "ListItem",
        position: index + 1,
        name: product.name,
        url: new URL(`#${product.id}`, Astro.url).toString(),
      })),
    },
    ...PRODUCTS.map((product) => ({
      "@type": "Product",
      name: product.name,
      description: product.description,
      brand: {
        "@type": "Brand",
        name: product.name.split(" ")[0],
      },
    })),
  ],
};
---

<Layout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: dates.published,
    modifiedTime: dates.modified,
    author: authorName(Astro.url.pathname),
    section: "Home Improvement",
    tags: [
      "deck stain sprayer",
      "sprayer for deck stain",
      "best stain sprayer",
      "pump sprayer for deck stain",
      "airless sprayer for deck stain",
      "spraying deck stain"
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <!-- Hero Section -->
    <div class="bg-gray-900 py-16 lg:py-24 relative overflow-hidden">
      <div class="absolute inset-0 opacity-10" style="background-image: radial-gradient(circle at 20% 20%, #38BDF8 0, transparent 45%), radial-gradient(circle at 80% 60%, #FF4500 0, transparent 40%);"></div>
      <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 relative z-10 text-center">
        <div class="flex justify-center mb-6">
          <TrustBadge />
        </div>
        <div class="flex justify-center gap-3 mb-6">
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-[#FF4500]/20 text-[#FF4500] text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest border border-[#FF4500]/30">
            Buyer's Guide
          </span>
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-green-500/10 text-green-400 text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest border border-green-500/20">
            Updated November 2026
          </span>
        </div>
        <h1 class="text-4xl lg:text-6xl font-black text-white leading-tight mb-8">
          The Best <span class="text-[#38BDF8]">Deck Stain Sprayers</span>
        </h1>
        <p class="text-xl text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-8 max-w-3xl mx-auto">
          Most deck stain is thin enough for a cheap pump sprayer, and an airless set up for house paint will waste half of it into the air. Here are the six sprayers and tips worth buying, matched to the stain in your can.
        </p>
        <div class="flex flex-col sm:flex-row gap-4 justify-center">
          <a
            href="#chapin-25012-clean-n-seal"
            class="px-8 py-4 bg-[#FF4500] hover:bg-[#E63E00] text-white font-black rounded-xl shadow-lg transition-all no-underline"
          >
            See Our #1 Pick
          </a>
          <a
            href="#top-picks"
            class="px-8 py-4 bg-white/10 hover:bg-white/20 text-white font-bold rounded-xl border border-white/10 no-underline"
          >
            See the Picks
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Author & Meta -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pt-12">
      <div class="flex items-center gap-6 border-b border-gray-100 pb-8">
        <div class="flex items-center gap-3">
          <AuthorAvatar size={48} />
          <div>
            <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Alex Rivers</p>
            <p class="text-xs text-gray-500 uppercase tracking-widest">Home Improvement Editor</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="h-10 w-px bg-gray-200 hidden sm:block"></div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block">
          <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Last Updated</p>
          <p class="text-xs text-gray-500">{dates.display}</p>
        </div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block ml-auto">
          <SocialProof users="7.1k" />
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Table of Contents -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
      <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-black text-gray-900 mb-4 flex items-center gap-2">
          In This Guide
        </h2>
        <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2">
          <a href="#which-type" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">1. Which Sprayer Your Stain Needs</a>
          <a href="#top-picks" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">2. The 6 Best, Ranked</a>
          <a href="#pump" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">3. Pump Sprayers</a>
          <a href="#airless" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">4. Airless and Low-Pressure Tips</a>
          <a href="#hvlp" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">5. HVLP Limitations</a>
          <a href="#back-brush" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">6. Back-Brushing</a>
          <a href="#overspray" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">7. Controlling Overspray</a>
          <a href="#setup" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">8. Setup and Cleaning</a>
          <a href="#skip" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">9. Who Should Not Buy One</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">10. FAQ</a>
          <a href="#mistakes" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">11. Common Mistakes</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          The best sprayer for deck stain is usually not the most powerful one. A thin penetrating stain sprays beautifully from a 1-gallon pump sprayer with a fan nozzle, and through a full-pressure airless it runs, fogs and drifts onto the siding. Pick the sprayer for the stain, not the deck, and plan to brush everything behind it.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="which-type" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Which Kind of Sprayer Your Stain Needs
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Start With What Is in the Can</h3>
          <p>
            The right deck stain sprayer depends almost entirely on what is in the can. Deck stains range from very thin penetrating oils, almost as runny as water, to solid stains that behave like thin paint. A sprayer that handles one well often handles the other badly, and buying an expensive airless to spray a thin oil is one of the most common ways people waste money on this job.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Thin Stains: A Pump Sprayer</h3>
          <p>
            For transparent, toner and semi-transparent stains, which are most of what people put on decks, a pump sprayer is the right tool. These stains are thin enough to flow through a pump sprayer's nozzle at 30 to 60 PSI, and a flat fan nozzle lays down a controlled stripe that is easy to back-brush. Pump sprayers are cheap, quiet, need no power and clean out with a tank of water or solvent.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Solid Stains: An Airless Sprayer</h3>
          <p>
            For solid and semi-solid stains, a pump sprayer usually is not enough. The thicker material clogs the nozzle, the pattern breaks up, and you end up dribbling stain rather than spraying it. That is where an airless sprayer earns its place, ideally one with adjustable pressure or a low-pressure tip so it does not atomize the stain into a cloud.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">HVLP: Railings and Detail Only</h3>
          <p>
