        <div id="pump" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Pump Sprayers: The Right Tool for Most Decks
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How a Pump Sprayer Works</h3>
          <p>
            A pump sprayer is a sealed tank you pressurize with a hand pump, a hose, a wand and a nozzle. That is all, and for penetrating stain it is all you need. The quality differences that matter are the nozzle, the seals and the tank size.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Fan Nozzle and Solvent-Resistant Seals</h3>
          <p>
            The nozzle should be a flat fan, not a cone. Most garden sprayers come with an adjustable cone nozzle designed to mist plants, which puts stain on the wood in an uneven circle and blows a lot of it into the air. A fan nozzle lays a straight stripe along a board or down a baluster that you can see, control and brush in. Deck-specific sprayers like the Chapin 25012 include fan nozzles in the box; for a general-purpose sprayer, buy or use the fan nozzle option.
          </p>

          <p>
            The seals matter for oil-based stain. The solvents in oil and alkyd stains attack the standard rubber seals in many cheap garden sprayers, which swell, leak and eventually stop the tank holding pressure. Viton or other chemical-resistant seals last much longer. If you are spraying water-based stain only, this matters less, but you still need to flush the sprayer promptly so the stain does not dry in the wand.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Tank Size</h3>
          <p>
            Tank size is a trade-off. A 1-gallon sprayer is light enough to carry along a railing with one hand and to hold at awkward angles under the top rail. A 2-gallon sprayer means half as many refills on a large floor but weighs well over 15 pounds full of stain. Many people end up liking the 1-gallon size for railings and a pad on a pole for the floor, which needs no sprayer at all.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Where Pump Sprayers Fall Short</h3>
          <p>
            Pump sprayers have limits. Pressure drops as you spray, so you pump every minute or two to keep the pattern consistent. They will not handle solid stain. And the spray is coarse enough that, unlike an airless, it puts stain exactly where you aim and not much further, which near a house is a real advantage.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="airless" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Airless Sprayers and Low-Pressure Tips
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How an Airless Sprayer Works</h3>
          <p>
            An airless sprayer pumps the coating at high pressure, typically 1,500 to 3,000 PSI, through a tiny tip that breaks it into a fine spray. It is the fastest way to coat a large area, and for solid stain on a big deck, railing and fence it can turn a weekend into an afternoon. It is also the sprayer most likely to cause problems with deck stain.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Built for Paint, Not Stain</h3>
          <p>
            The issue is that airless sprayers are designed for house paint. Paint is thick, so it needs high pressure to atomize, and a 515 tip, which gives roughly a 10-inch fan through a .015 orifice, is the standard size that ships with most units. Thin deck stain through that same setup sprays far too much material, too finely. It runs on vertical surfaces, puddles on the floor, and turns a meaningful share of the stain into mist that drifts onto siding, windows and the neighbor's car. Owners commonly report that their first attempt at spraying stain through an airless used much more stain than the can's coverage rate suggested.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Three Adjustments for Stain</h3>
          <p>
            Three adjustments fix most of that. First, turn the pressure down. Most quality airless sprayers, including the Graco Magnum X5, have a pressure control; run it at the lowest setting that gives a fan without heavy tails at the edges. Second, use a smaller tip. A .009 to .011 orifice suits thin stain far better than a .015. Third, consider a low-pressure tip such as Graco's FFLP line, which Graco says sprays at up to 50 percent lower pressure with a softer fan. Wagner's HEA sprayers, like the Control Pro 130, are built around the same idea at the pump.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Back-Brushing and Cleanup</h3>
          <p>
            Even set up well, an airless puts down a lot of stain fast, so you need a second person or a very disciplined routine to back-brush behind it before it starts to dry. Cleanup is also real work: the pump, hose, filter and gun all need flushing after every session, and stain left in a hose overnight can ruin it. If you will only use a sprayer once, our <a href="/paint-sprayer-rental/">paint sprayer rental guide</a> compares the cost of renting an airless against buying one.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Turn the pressure down, go to a smaller tip, and back-brush everything.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">An airless set up for house paint will over-apply deck stain and fill the air with mist. Low pressure and a small orifice fix most of it; a brush behind the gun fixes the rest.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="hvlp" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Why HVLP Struggles on Deck Floors
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How HVLP Works</h3>
          <p>
            HVLP stands for high volume, low pressure. A turbine pushes a large volume of air through the gun at only a few PSI, and the material is drawn into that airstream and atomized gently. The result is a soft, easily controlled spray with very little overspray, which is why HVLP is the standard for cabinets, furniture and fine finishing.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Slow Output and Viscosity Limits</h3>
          <p>
            On a deck, the same properties become limitations. HVLP lays down material slowly, typically a fraction of what an airless delivers, and most consumer units spray from a cup of a quart or so. The FLEXiO 595 in our picks has a 1.5-quart cup. A 300 square foot floor in two coats can take several gallons of stain, which means a dozen or more refills and a lot of time holding a gun with a motor in it. A pad on a pole is faster on a floor.
          </p>

          <p>
            HVLP also works best with materials of a consistent, fairly low viscosity. Thin penetrating stains spray well; thick solid stains often need thinning, and many stain manufacturers do not want their products thinned. Read the can before you add anything to it.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Where HVLP Does Make Sense</h3>
          <p>
            Where HVLP does make sense is on intricate vertical work close to the house: turned spindles, lattice skirting, pergola slats and outdoor furniture. There, low overspray and fine control are worth more than speed. If you already own an HVLP for indoor projects, use it for the railings. If you do not, a pump sprayer with a fan nozzle and a spray shield gets you most of the same control for much less money.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="back-brush" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Back-Brushing: The Step a Sprayer Does Not Replace
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Spraying Puts Stain On, Not In</h3>
          <p>
            A sprayer is a fast way to put stain on wood. It is not a way to put stain into wood. Sprayed stain sits on the surface, beads in some spots, misses the shaded side of rough grain and collects in puddles where boards cup. If it is left like that, the puddles stay tacky, the thin areas weather first, and the finish fails unevenly. Every sprayed surface needs to be brushed within a few minutes, while the stain is still wet.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What Back-Brushing Does</h3>
          <p>
            Back-brushing works the stain into the grain and the checks, spreads out heavy areas, pulls excess out of the gaps between boards and evens the color. On a floor, a block brush on an extension pole is the best tool; on railings, a 3-inch brush with bristle matched to the stain. Our <a href="/best-deck-stain-brush/">deck stain brush guide</a> covers which brush goes with which stain, and our <a href="/brush-vs-roller-vs-sprayer-deck-stain/">brush vs roller vs sprayer comparison</a> explains why every method needs the brush behind it.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">A Two-Person Job</h3>
          <p>
            The practical consequence is that spraying is a two-person job on anything larger than a small railing. One person sprays a few boards or a section of balusters, the other follows with the brush. Working alone, spray only as much as you can brush before it starts to dry, which on a warm, sunny day might be just two or three boards.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Remove the Excess</h3>
          <p>
            Penetrating stains need the excess wiped or brushed away, not left. If stain is still glossy on the surface 20 to 30 minutes after application, it is not going to soak in. Brush it out or wipe it off, or it will stay sticky, attract dirt and peel. The can's label will tell you whether a product wants a second wet-on-wet coat or a single coat with the excess removed.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="overspray" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Controlling Overspray Near the House
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Why Overspray Matters</h3>
          <p>
            Overspray is the reason many people regret spraying a deck. Deck stain is designed to soak into porous surfaces, and it soaks into vinyl siding texture, brick, concrete and painted trim just as readily. Once it has dried there it is hard to remove; our guide on <a href="/how-to-remove-deck-stain-from-vinyl-siding/">removing deck stain from vinyl siding</a> exists because this happens so often.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Mask First</h3>
          <p>
            Start by masking. Tape plastic sheeting to the house wall for at least the first few feet above the deck, cover windows and doors, and lay drop cloths over anything on the ground below the deck edge, including plants, the patio and the air conditioner. Move cars. On an airless, plan for mist to travel well past where you think it will.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Match the Sprayer to the Location</h3>
          <p>
            Then choose the sprayer to suit the location. A pump sprayer with a fan nozzle and a spray shield produces almost no drifting mist and is the safest option next to siding. HVLP is next. An HEA airless like the Wagner Control Pro 130 is a large improvement over conventional airless. A conventional airless at full pressure is the most likely to put stain on something you did not intend.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Watch the Weather</h3>
          <p>
            Watch the weather. Wind of even 5 to 10 miles per hour carries spray a long way, and direct sun dries stain before you can brush it. Spray early in the morning or late afternoon on a calm day, in the temperature range on the can. Our guide to <a href="/what-temperature-to-stain-a-deck/">what temperature to stain a deck</a> covers the window. And spray downward and away from the house whenever you can, working from the wall outward so you never have to reach back over a wet section.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="setup" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Setting Up and Cleaning a Stain Sprayer
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Strain the Stain</h3>
          <p>
            Whatever sprayer you use, strain the stain first. Old stain, or stain that has sat open, develops skins and lumps that block nozzles and tips. A paint strainer bag over the bucket costs very little and saves most clogs. Stir the stain thoroughly; pigment settles, and an unstirred can gives uneven color across the deck.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Test the Pattern</h3>
          <p>
            Test the pattern on a scrap board or a piece of cardboard before you start on the deck. You are looking for an even fan with soft edges, no spitting and no heavy tails. On a pump sprayer, pump up, spray and note how many strokes it takes to keep the pattern steady. On an airless, start at low pressure and increase until the tails disappear, then stop.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Clean It Right Away</h3>
          <p>
            Clean the sprayer as soon as you finish. For water-based stain, flush a pump sprayer with clean water until it runs clear through the nozzle; for oil-based stain, flush with mineral spirits or the cleaner on the can, then with soapy water. Release the pressure before you open the tank. On an airless, follow the manufacturer's flushing procedure through the pump, hose and gun, and remove and clean the filters and tip. A sprayer left with stain in it overnight is often a sprayer that needs new seals.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Dispose of Rags Safely</h3>
          <p>
            Dispose of solvent-soaked rags safely. Rags used with oil-based stain can heat up as they dry and catch fire. Lay them flat outdoors to dry, or seal them in a metal can of water, and check local rules for disposing of leftover stain and solvent.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="skip" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Who Should Not Buy a Sprayer
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Small, Simple Decks</h3>
          <p>
            A sprayer is not automatically faster for a deck. On a small, simple deck of 150 to 250 square feet with a plain railing, the time you spend masking, setting up and cleaning a sprayer can exceed the time it saves. A pad on a pole for the floor and a brush for the railing will get the job done in about the same time with less mess and less waste.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Where Spraying Pays Off</h3>
          <p>
            Spraying makes the most sense when there is a lot of vertical surface: many balusters, lattice skirting, a long fence, or a two-story deck with stairs. There, the sprayer's speed on fiddly surfaces pays for its setup time. It also makes sense if you will reuse the sprayer for a fence, cleaner and brightener, or regular restaining every two to three years.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Buy the Cheapest Sprayer That Suits Your Stain</h3>
          <p>
            If you buy one, buy the cheapest sprayer that suits your stain. For penetrating stains that is a pump sprayer, and the Chapin 25012 is our pick. Only move to an airless if you are applying solid stain over a large area, and fit a low-pressure tip when you do.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Prep Comes First</h3>
          <p>
            And whatever you spray, prepare the deck properly first. No sprayer compensates for a deck that is dirty, mildewed or covered in failing old stain. Our <a href="/how-to-prep-a-deck-for-staining/">deck prep guide</a> covers cleaning, stripping and brightening in the right order, and our <a href="/best-solid-deck-stain/">solid deck stain guide</a> covers which opaque products spray well.
          </p>
        </div>

        <PlanTheJob />

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            10. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best sprayer for deck stain?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For transparent and semi-transparent stains, which are most deck stains, a pump sprayer with a flat fan nozzle such as the Chapin 25012 is the best choice. It is cheap, controllable and produces little overspray. For solid stain over a large area, an airless sprayer with adjustable pressure or a low-pressure tip, such as the Graco Magnum X5, is better.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you use a pump sprayer for deck stain?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. A pump sprayer is the most common and often the best way to spray penetrating deck stain. Use a flat fan nozzle rather than a cone, choose chemical-resistant seals for oil-based stain, and flush the sprayer right after use. Pump sprayers struggle with solid stain, which is usually too thick for their nozzles.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is an airless sprayer good for deck stain?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It can be, especially for solid stain on large decks and fences, but it needs setting up for stain. Turn the pressure down, use a smaller tip than the paint tip it ships with, and consider a low-pressure fine finish tip. Mask the house carefully, because airless overspray drifts, and back-brush everything behind the gun.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you spray deck stain with an HVLP sprayer?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                You can, and HVLP gives the least overspray and finest control, which suits spindles, lattice and furniture. But it is slow and most consumer units have a cup of about a quart, so it is impractical for a deck floor. Use it for detailed railings and use a pad, brush or pump sprayer for the boards.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do you have to back-brush after spraying deck stain?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. Spraying puts stain on the surface; back-brushing works it into the grain, spreads out puddles, pulls excess from the gaps and evens the color. Without it, heavy areas stay tacky and thin areas fail early. On anything larger than a small railing, spraying and brushing is easiest as a two-person job.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What tip size should I use to spray deck stain?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For thin penetrating stains through an airless, a small orifice such as .009 to .011 works better than the .015 tips most sprayers ship with. A narrow fan suits balusters and pickets; a wider fan suits floor boards. Low-pressure tips like Graco's FFLP line reduce overspray further. For solid stain, follow the stain maker's tip recommendation.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do I stop overspray when spraying a deck?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Mask the house wall, windows and the ground below the deck before you start. Use a pump sprayer with a spray shield near siding, or an airless at low pressure with a low-pressure tip. Spray on a calm day, aim down and away from the house, and work from the wall outward so you never reach over wet stain.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying an airless for a penetrating stain</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Thin transparent and semi-transparent stains spray well from a pump sprayer costing a fraction of an airless. Through an airless at paint pressure they over-apply, run and fog. Unless you are spraying solid stain over a large area, a pump sprayer with a fan nozzle is the better tool.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Spraying without back-brushing</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Sprayed stain sits on the surface and puddles where boards cup. Left alone it stays tacky and fails unevenly. Brush every sprayed surface within a few minutes, while it is still wet, to work the stain into the grain and pull excess out of the gaps.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using the paint tip that came with the airless</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A 515 tip is sized for house paint. Thin stain through it sprays far too much material. Drop to a smaller orifice, turn the pressure down to the lowest setting that gives a clean fan, and consider a low-pressure fine finish tip.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Not masking the house</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Deck stain soaks into siding, brick and concrete as readily as wood, and is hard to remove once dry. Tape plastic to the wall above the deck, cover windows, and drop-cloth the ground below. Use a spray shield with a pump sprayer near siding.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Spraying on a windy or hot day</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Even light wind carries spray well beyond the deck, and direct sun dries stain before you can brush it. Spray early or late on a calm day, within the temperature range on the can, and work small sections so the brush can keep up.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

